                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "238329927",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198129636589",
                            "num_reviews": 18,
                            "playtime_forever": 3750,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 2457,
                            "playtime_at_review": 1165,
                            "last_played": 1794017534
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I've grown annoyed at how most modern platformers handle difficulty and punishment. Most games give you checkpoints every 10 or so seconds of gameplay meaning you only have to be good enough to get past a handful of jumps at a time. Dustforce requires you to be perfect throughout the whole level in order to progress, often requiring more than a minute of well timed execution. The controls are legitimately hard to master and you might completely suck at the start, which means the skill difference between beginners and experts are extreme. The level design is top notch, the hardest levels often required me to solve tricky non-obvious scenarios. I found myself growing specific skill sets and discovering small bits of tech throughout the entire game.\nIf you think level design shouldn't require advanced routing, or that controls need to be ultra-intuitive and basic so that beginners can play well, or dislike being punished greatly for screwups, probably don't pick up this game. If legitimate difficulty is something you've been craving Dustforce will probably be your favorite game of all time.\nTime at review is how long it took me to perfect rank all levels in the main campaign (all levels in Nexus) with checkpoints permanently off.",
